    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mandela joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> in 1943, moved from non-violent protest to co-founding the armed group <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umkhonto-we-sizwe">Umkhonto we Sizwe</a> in 1961, and was sentenced to life imprisonment in 1964.</li><li>Released in 1990 after 27 years, he negotiated an end to apartheid with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a> and led the ANC to victory in the multiracial election of 1994.</li><li>As president he made reconciliation between the country's racial groups his primary task, while critics on the left and right, and later he himself, faulted parts of his record.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>While a student at the University of Fort Hare, Mandela performed in a drama society play about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>. He left the university without a degree after being suspended for joining a boycott over the quality of the food.</p></aside>
    </section>

